The BC-250 Mesa patch series is vendored from upstream, unchanged, in one
directory per Mesa release it was rebased for:

  patches/26.2.4 @ f05991ace64a42eda3d2dc583e96b8d35ce393e3
  patches/26.2.5 @ c771c1f7d32337d8b972960c678f58cebee13ff0
  patches/26.2.6 @ 4199d5b5c5b547ba8e90403ef1808f2123014341

Each directory holds the same seven patches:

  0001-gfx1013-compute-queue-fix.patch          expose the ACE compute queue
  0002-bc250-directmesh.patch                   DirectMesh v1.3.1 (mesh/task shaders)
  0003-bc250-fsr4-v3.patch                      FSR4: deferred SDot lowering
  0004-bc250-fsr4-combined-unroll.patch         FSR4: combined unroll/rematerialize
  0005-bc250-fsr4-imageprep-texture.patch       FSR4: image prep and texture
  0006-bc250-fsr4-resolution-variants.patch     FSR4: 1080p and 4K+ shader buckets
  0007-bc250-fsr4-production-defaults.patch     FSR4: turn the candidates on

The series is per Mesa release because the BC-250 series funds its shader
key flags out of padding bits that upstream Mesa hands out as it needs
them. Mesa 26.2.5 took one of them for the exact-GLSL-FMA option, so the
26.2.4 series does not apply to 26.2.5 and the 26.2.5 series does not
apply to 26.2.4. The 26.2.5 series is upstream's "fund DirectMesh's key
bits for 26.2.5" revision, which names the leftover key bits, moves the
route-key mirrors to 28 bytes and bumps RADV_BC250_PIPELINE_PLAN_VERSION to
9 so a plan serialized by the old layout is not read as the new one.

The 26.2.6 series is upstream's patches/mesa at 4199d5b5c5b5, the newest
revision of the stable series. Upstream had not keyed a rebase for 26.2.6
at the time of vendoring (its stable Mesa target is still 26.2.5); beyond
c771c1f7 the series gained the DirectMesh auto-IDX/RDNA1 release
(d9fb8378580b) and an explicit pad in the IDX setup args for the 32-bit
builds (4199d5b5c5b5), while the FSR4 patches changed only in their
index blob-hash lines. This repository verified the series for 26.2.6
before vendoring it: every patch applies cleanly to the pristine Mesa
26.2.6 tarball in build order (series, then patches/local, then the OGC
radv-defaults with the reduced-context fallback and vram-overcommit
strictly, each reverse-verified), and the files that hold the shader key
bits (nir_shader_compiler_options.h, radv_shader.h, radv_pipeline.h,
radv_pipeline_graphics.h) are byte-identical between 26.2.5 and 26.2.6,
so 26.2.6 spent none of the padding bits the series is funded from.

patches/local/0100-radv-ac-optional-bc250-ip-debug-print.patch is this
image's own diagnostic aid and is not from upstream. It applies on top of
every vendored series.

The helpers, the correctness tests and the soak test are vendored unchanged
from the previous source, https://github.com/tri3gubki-ops/bc250-async-compute-bazzite
at commit 56bf06b986f4639c32a669e9cdd31cdf7fba44b6 (MIT, see LICENSE). The
image build relocates /usr/local paths into immutable /usr paths.

Mesa is not built from a pinned tarball. The build stage starts from the
base image and uses the base image's own Mesa version, so the private driver
tracks the Mesa Bazzite ships. The OpenGamingCollective patches (radv-defaults,
vram-overcommit) are fetched for that version and applied, so the private
driver keeps the same features as the system driver.

Patch order matters and is fixed by the build script: the vendored series is
applied first, then the OpenGamingCollective patches. OGC's vram-overcommit
patch inserts into radv_amdgpu_bo.c where DirectMesh's first hunk expects the
pristine file, so the reverse order conflicts.
